    "devanagari": "प्रत्यक्षानुमानोपमानशब्दाः प्रमाणानि ॥",
    "iast": "pratyakṣānumānopamānaśabdāḥ pramāṇāni",
    "translation": "Perception (Pratyaksha), Inference (Anumana), Analogy / Comparison (Upamana), and Verbal Testimony (Shabda) are the means of right knowledge (Pramanas).",
    "commentary": "The preliminary survey and specific enumeration of the fourfold epistemic instruments of Nyāya, expounded through the introductory Bhāṣya and Vārtika:\n\n1. The Threefold Scientific Method (Trividha-Śāstra-Pravṛtti): Vātsyāyana lays down that scientific philosophical inquiry proceeds strictly by three stages: Enunciation (Uddeśa: naming the categories), Definition (Lakṣaṇa: stating the essential differentiating mark), and Critical Examination (Parīkṣā: dialectical investigation using reasoning to test whether the definition holds true). Sūtra 1.1.1 gave the general enunciation; Sūtra 1.1.3 provides the specific classification (Vibhāga).\n\n2. Why Specific Enumeration is Essential (Parisaṅkhyā): An opponent asks: If Sūtra 1 already enunciated 'Pramāṇa', why enumerate them in Sūtra 3? Uddyotakara clarifies that Definition (Lakṣaṇa) can only differentiate an object from other things, but can never restrict or specify numerical quantity (saṅkhyā-niyama). Without explicit enumeration ('they are four only'), a seeker would constantly doubt whether other valid instruments exist. Enumeration settles this doubt decisively.\n\n3. Etymology & Instrument-Result Relation (Pramāṇa-Phala-Bhāva): The word 'Pramāṇa' is derived from root 'mā' (to measure/know) with prefix 'pra' and instrumental suffix 'lyuṭ': 'pramīyate anena iti pramāṇam' (that by means of which reality is accurately known). When the pramāṇa is viewed as the cognitive instrument (such as sense-organ contact), its result (phala) is the resulting cognition (pramiti). When the pramāṇa is viewed as the cognition itself, its practical result is the behavioral disposition: Rejection (Hāna), Acquisition (Upādāna), or Indifference (Upekṣā).\n\n4. Preliminary Definitions of the Four Means:\n- Perception (Pratyakṣa): Sense-organ functioning on an object (akṣasya akṣasya prativiṣayaṃ vṛttiḥ); grammatically a prādi-compound (pratigatam akṣam).\n- Inference (Anumāna): The after-cognition (anu-māna) of the probandum (sādhya) through the recognized indicative sign (liṅga/hetu).\n- Analogy (Upamāna): The cognition of similarity (sādhṛśya-jñāna) leading to knowledge of the connection between a name and the named (saṃjñā-saṃjñi-sambandha).\n- Word (Śabda): Instructive verbal assertion by which objects are denoted and communicated.\n\n5. Scope (Pramāṇa-Saṃplava vs. Pramāṇa-Vyavasthā): Pramāṇas operate both convergently and exclusively. In Pramāṇa-Saṃplava, multiple instruments grasp the same object: the Soul and Fire are known by testimony, inferred by indicative marks, and directly perceived by yogic or visual contact. In Pramāṇa-Vyavasthā, an object is accessible to one instrument alone: Heaven (Svarga) through Vedic Word alone; the source of thunder through Inference alone; one's own hand through Perception alone.\n\n6. The Supreme Primacy of Perception (Pratyakṣa-Jyaiṣṭhya): Perception is the sovereign foundation of all pramāṇas. Inference and Analogy presuppose prior perceptual data, and Verbal Testimony requires auditory perception of words. Most crucially, Perception alone quenches the epistemic craving of the inquirer (jijñāsā-nivṛtti): hearing of fire creates a desire to infer it; inferring fire prompts the urge to see it; but once perceived directly with the eyes, all epistemic longing comes to complete rest.",
